  "worked_solution": "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{\u2033}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{\u2032}(x) & =4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}+48x-6 \\\\ {g}^{\u2033}(x) & =12{x}^{2}+60x+48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{\u2033}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}+60x+48 & =0 \\\\ 12(x+4)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-4$ and $x=-1.$ So we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-5,\\,$ $x=-2,\\,$ and $x=0,$\n\n and compute the sign of ${g}^{\u2033}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{\u2033}(-5) & =12(-5)^{2}+60(-5)+48=48>0 \\\\ {g}^{\u2033}(-2) & =12(-2)^{2}+60(-2)+48=-24<0 \\\\ {g}^{\u2033}(0) & =12(0)^{2}+60(0)+48=48>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=-4$ and $x=-1.$Therefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-4$ and $x=-1,$ we conclude that $x=-4$ and $x=-1$ are indeed  points of inflection.\n\nTherefore,  $x=-4$ and $x=-1$ are points of inflection.",
